### What is Apache Pulsar?

Apache Pulsar is a distributed messaging and streaming platform whose defining feature is a decoupled architecture that separates serving from storage. Pulsar brokers are stateless routers that handle client connections and message routing but store nothing locally. Durable storage is delegated to Apache BookKeeper nodes called bookies, and cluster metadata lives in a separate metadata store such as ZooKeeper or etcd.

This architectural separation means you can add or remove brokers in seconds without any data movement. In a banking context, when National Bank of India experiences a festival trading spike, new Pulsar brokers can be spun up instantly to handle the load without rebalancing any data.

```java
package com.bank.pulsar;

import org.apache.pulsar.client.api.PulsarClient;
import org.apache.pulsar.client.api.Producer;
import org.apache.pulsar.client.api.Schema;

public class PulsarTransferProducer {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        PulsarClient client = PulsarClient.builder()
            .serviceUrl("pulsar://localhost:6650")
            .build();

        // Multi-tenant topic naming: tenant/namespace/topic
        Producer<String> producer = client.newProducer(Schema.STRING)
            .topic("persistent://banking-tenant/payments/transfers")
            .create();

        producer.send("{\"accountId\":\"SAV2001\",\"amount\":50000}");
        System.out.println("Published transfer to Pulsar");

        producer.close();
        client.close();
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Published transfer to Pulsar
Topic: persistent://banking-tenant/payments/transfers
```

### How does Pulsar's architecture differ from Kafka?

The defining difference is the separation of compute and storage. In Kafka, a broker both serves clients and stores partition log segments on its own disk. When you add a broker to scale, Kafka must physically rebalance partition data across the network, which is slow and I/O heavy. In Pulsar, brokers are stateless routers that own no data. Storage is handled by BookKeeper bookies that store message segments (ledgers) spread across many nodes.

| Feature | Kafka | Pulsar |
|---|---|---|
| Broker role | Serve clients + store data | Serve clients only (stateless) |
| Storage | Broker-local disk (coupled) | BookKeeper bookies (decoupled) |
| Scaling brokers | Requires partition reassignment | Instant (no data movement) |
| Scaling storage | Add broker + reassign partitions | Add bookies independently |
| Data unit | Partition log segments | Ledger segments on bookies |
| Metadata store | KRaft (internal) or ZooKeeper | ZooKeeper or etcd |

```java
package com.bank.comparison;

public class ArchitectureComparison {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Kafka Architecture:");
        System.out.println("  Broker 1: serves clients + stores partition 0, 1");
        System.out.println("  Broker 2: serves clients + stores partition 2, 3");
        System.out.println("  Adding Broker 3: must reassign partitions (slow)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Pulsar Architecture:");
        System.out.println("  Broker 1: serves clients (no data)");
        System.out.println("  Broker 2: serves clients (no data)");
        System.out.println("  Bookie 1: stores ledger segments");
        System.out.println("  Bookie 2: stores ledger segments");
        System.out.println("  Adding Broker 3: instant (no data to move)");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Kafka Architecture:
  Broker 1: serves clients + stores partition 0, 1
  Broker 2: serves clients + stores partition 2, 3
  Adding Broker 3: must reassign partitions (slow)

Pulsar Architecture:
  Broker 1: serves clients (no data)
  Broker 2: serves clients (no data)
  Bookie 1: stores ledger segments
  Bookie 2: stores ledger segments
  Adding Broker 3: instant (no data to move)
```

### What is Pulsar's multi-tenancy model?

Multi-tenancy is first-class in Pulsar through the tenant/namespace/topic hierarchy. A topic name looks like `persistent://banking-tenant/payments/transfers`. The tenant is an organization, the namespace is a grouping with its own policies (retention, quotas, access control), and the topic is the stream. This lets one Pulsar cluster safely host many isolated business units. Kafka has no native tenant concept; isolation is usually done with separate clusters or naming conventions plus ACLs.

```java
package com.bank.pulsar;

public class MultiTenancyExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Pulsar Multi-Tenancy at National Bank of India:");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Tenant: retail-banking");
        System.out.println("  Namespace: payments  (retention: 7 days, quota: 100GB)");
        System.out.println("    Topic: persistent://retail-banking/payments/transfers");
        System.out.println("    Topic: persistent://retail-banking/payments/bills");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Tenant: wealth-management");
        System.out.println("  Namespace: trading  (retention: 30 days, quota: 500GB)");
        System.out.println("    Topic: persistent://wealth-management/trading/orders");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Tenant: fintech-partner");
        System.out.println("  Namespace: api  (retention: 3 days, quota: 10GB)");
        System.out.println("    Topic: persistent://fintech-partner/api/requests");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("All tenants isolated on ONE Pulsar cluster.");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Pulsar Multi-Tenancy at National Bank of India:

Tenant: retail-banking
  Namespace: payments  (retention: 7 days, quota: 100GB)
    Topic: persistent://retail-banking/payments/transfers
    Topic: persistent://retail-banking/payments/bills

Tenant: wealth-management
  Namespace: trading  (retention: 30 days, quota: 500GB)
    Topic: persistent://wealth-management/trading/orders

Tenant: fintech-partner
  Namespace: api  (retention: 3 days, quota: 10GB)
    Topic: persistent://fintech-partner/api/requests

All tenants isolated on ONE Pulsar cluster.
```

### How does Pulsar handle subscription types?

Pulsar natively supports multiple subscription types in the same topic. Exclusive and Failover subscriptions behave like streaming (one active consumer per subscription). Shared and Key_Shared subscriptions behave like a traditional queue where multiple consumers compete for messages. Kafka only offers the consumer-group model, which is essentially a log-based competing consumer within partitions.

| Subscription Type | Behavior | Kafka Equivalent |
|---|---|---|
| Exclusive | One consumer, fails if another joins | Single-partition topic |
| Failover | Active-standby, auto-failover | Consumer group with 1 partition |
| Shared | Round-robin across consumers (queue) | No direct equivalent |
| Key_Shared | Same key to same consumer (ordered) | Consumer group with key partitioning |

```java
package com.bank.pulsar;

import org.apache.pulsar.client.api.Consumer;
import org.apache.pulsar.client.api.PulsarClient;
import org.apache.pulsar.client.api.Schema;
import org.apache.pulsar.client.api.SubscriptionType;

public class SharedSubscriptionConsumer {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        PulsarClient client = PulsarClient.builder()
            .serviceUrl("pulsar://localhost:6650")
            .build();

        // Shared subscription: multiple consumers compete for messages
        // like a traditional queue
        Consumer<String> consumer = client.newConsumer(Schema.STRING)
            .topic("persistent://banking-tenant/payments/transfers")
            .subscriptionName("settlement-sub")
            .subscriptionType(SubscriptionType.Shared)
            .subscribe();

        System.out.println("Shared consumer listening for transfers...");
        // Multiple instances compete for messages
        consumer.close();
        client.close();
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Shared consumer listening for transfers...
-> Instance A processes message 1
-> Instance B processes message 2
-> Instance A processes message 3 (round-robin distribution)
```

### What about Pulsar's built-in geo-replication?

Geo-replication is built into Pulsar at the namespace level. You enable replication to another cluster with a configuration change, and Pulsar handles the cross-region copy transparently. Kafka achieves this with the separate MirrorMaker 2 tool, which is powerful but operationally heavier because you deploy and manage an additional replication cluster.

```java
package com.bank.pulsar;

public class GeoReplicationExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Pulsar Geo-Replication:");
        System.out.println("  Namespace: banking-tenant/payments");
        System.out.println("  Replicated clusters: mumbai, chennai");
        System.out.println("  Configuration: one admin API call");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Kafka Geo-Replication:");
        System.out.println("  Tool: MirrorMaker 2 (separate deployment)");
        System.out.println("  Source: mumbai-cluster / transfer-events");
        System.out.println("  Target: chennai-cluster / mumbai.transfer-events");
        System.out.println("  Configuration: separate Connect cluster + config");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Pulsar Geo-Replication:
  Namespace: banking-tenant/payments
  Replicated clusters: mumbai, chennai
  Configuration: one admin API call

Kafka Geo-Replication:
  Tool: MirrorMaker 2 (separate deployment)
  Source: mumbai-cluster / transfer-events
  Target: chennai-cluster / mumbai.transfer-events
  Configuration: separate Connect cluster + config
```

### When would you still prefer Kafka over Pulsar?

Kafka has a far more mature ecosystem: Kafka Connect for hundreds of source and sink connectors, Kafka Streams for stream processing, ksqlDB for SQL on streams, Schema Registry for schema evolution, and enormous community and vendor support. If your organization already runs Kafka and has the operational expertise, and you need the highest raw sequential throughput on existing infrastructure, Kafka is the pragmatic choice.

```java
package com.bank.decision;

public class KafkaVsPulsarDecision {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Choose Kafka when:");
        System.out.println("  - Organization already runs Kafka");
        System.out.println("  - Need Kafka Connect, Streams, ksqlDB");
        System.out.println("  - Highest raw sequential throughput");
        System.out.println("  - Large community and vendor support");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Choose Pulsar when:");
        System.out.println("  - Need rapid elastic scaling (festival spikes)");
        System.out.println("  - Need multi-tenancy (retail + wealth + fintech)");
        System.out.println("  - Need built-in geo-replication");
        System.out.println("  - Need both stream and queue in one platform");
        System.out.println("  - Need built-in retry + DLT topics");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Choose Kafka when:
  - Organization already runs Kafka
  - Need Kafka Connect, Streams, ksqlDB
  - Highest raw sequential throughput
  - Large community and vendor support

Choose Pulsar when:
  - Need rapid elastic scaling (festival spikes)
  - Need multi-tenancy (retail + wealth + fintech)
  - Need built-in geo-replication
  - Need both stream and queue in one platform
  - Need built-in retry + DLT topics
```

### What are common mistakes when comparing Kafka and Pulsar?

The most common mistake is treating Pulsar as a drop-in replacement for Kafka without considering the operational cost of running BookKeeper. Another mistake is dismissing Pulsar entirely because Kafka is more popular, when Pulsar's architectural advantages may be a better fit.

```java
package com.bank.decision;

// WRONG: Assuming Pulsar is always better because of decoupled storage.
// You now operate TWO systems: Pulsar brokers AND BookKeeper bookies.
// If your team has Kafka expertise and no need for multi-tenancy,
// the migration cost outweighs the architectural benefit.

// CORRECT: Evaluate based on actual requirements.
public class PulsarMistakes {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("WRONG: Migrate to Pulsar just because it is newer.");
        System.out.println("  Cost: retrain team, operate BookKeeper, lose");
        System.out.println("  Kafka Connect/Streams/ksqlDB ecosystem.");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("CORRECT: Migrate to Pulsar when requirements demand it.");
        System.out.println("  - Multi-tenancy needed for isolated divisions");
        System.out.println("  - Elastic scaling needed for unpredictable spikes");
        System.out.println("  - Both queue and stream semantics on same cluster");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
WRONG: Migrate to Pulsar just because it is newer.
  Cost: retrain team, operate BookKeeper, lose
  Kafka Connect/Streams/ksqlDB ecosystem.

CORRECT: Migrate to Pulsar when requirements demand it.
  - Multi-tenancy needed for isolated divisions
  - Elastic scaling needed for unpredictable spikes
  - Both queue and stream semantics on same cluster
```

### What should a senior engineer know about this comparison?

A senior engineer should understand that Pulsar and Kafka serve overlapping but not identical needs. The decoupled architecture is not free: you trade Kafka's simpler single-system deployment for Pulsar's extra BookKeeper layer. The right answer depends on organizational context, not just technical features. Kafka's tiered storage feature is narrowing the storage flexibility gap, while Pulsar's ecosystem is maturing but still trails Kafka.

```java
package com.bank.senior;

public class SeniorPerspective {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Senior Engineer Assessment:");
        System.out.println("1. Architecture: Pulsar decoupled > Kafka coupled");
        System.out.println("   But: Kafka tiered storage narrows the gap");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("2. Ecosystem: Kafka >> Pulsar");
        System.out.println("   Connect, Streams, ksqlDB, Schema Registry");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("3. Operations: Kafka = 1 system, Pulsar = 2 systems");
        System.out.println("   (brokers + bookies + metadata store)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("4. Throughput: Kafka wins raw sequential throughput");
        System.out.println("   Pulsar wins elastic burst scaling");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Senior Engineer Assessment:
1. Architecture: Pulsar decoupled > Kafka coupled
   But: Kafka tiered storage narrows the gap

2. Ecosystem: Kafka >> Pulsar
   Connect, Streams, ksqlDB, Schema Registry

3. Operations: Kafka = 1 system, Pulsar = 2 systems
   (brokers + bookies + metadata store)

4. Throughput: Kafka wins raw sequential throughput
   Pulsar wins elastic burst scaling
```
